A||N||7||grgoyl86||Duke Rebuild - not hop-up||||10||
Z||000000||grgoyl86||09-29-2001||10:39 PM||||Hi Folks,<P>I've read all the discussion on hopping up the Iron Duke, and have seen the negative comments on this.<P>But, I'm not looking for 200 HP, just a nice reliable rebuild on an 87 DIS Duke. Maybe even get another HP or two out of it in the process.<P>This car has over 160,000 miles on it, it's been pretty reliable. But, it's getting noisier, and now has some blow-by.<P>I have just pulled an engine out of another 87 coupe, and plan to rebuild that one, having it ready to swap in.<P>So, I'm looking for suggestions. Does it make sense to do a rebuild myself, or would it make more sense to send it to a rebuilder?<P>Any advice anyone would like to give would be appreciated - any tips, experiences, etc.<P>Thanks a bunch!<P>Bob||||reg||10
Z||000001||My7Fieros||09-29-2001||11:04 PM||||If your relatively mechanically inclined, rebuild it yourself....you'll save yourself major $$$. Iron duke engines are incredibly easy to rebuild, and relatively cheap to. Good luck!<P>------------------<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><FONT COLOR="red"><B><I>My 7 Fieros</FONT></FONT></B></I>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fierowrecker||09-30-2001||01:39 AM||||Hey Bob!<BR>Plan on about $800 +/- to rebuild the 2.5...<BR>But beware, my 86 had quite a few broken roller lifters, as did the second 86 I bought...<BR>I ended up rebuilding an 84 so I could run standard lifters...<BR>By the time I was done, I spent enough to build a six!!!<BR>I am doing that at present, I bought a parts car with DIS 3800 for $100, and it runs!!!<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000003||foxgapfiero||09-30-2001||08:13 AM||||Bob, I'd tell you to talk to the Stickman, but he really does not use the DIS motor. The experince I had with the 88 that I built was very good. This one was siezed and had the head warped. We replaced one piston and rod ,all the bearings, had the head shaved and still got away under $500.00.  I'm going to do an 84 motor for my dying 86 SE next.<P>------------------<BR>Mark Hoagland<BR>86GT 3.4<BR>88GT T-Top<BR>85GT caddy v-8 next swap<BR>84SE trailer next project||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||09-30-2001||01:04 PM||||I'd try to find a motor out of a FWD car with low mileage... the DIS motor isn't hard to come by.<P>Fortunatly you are dealing with the DIS block... That casting is stronger than the older ones so the likely hood of a crack is pretty low.<P>Just what it will cost to rebuild one depends on how bad it is... It can be very dificult to redict that untill the thing is torn down.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000005||erikred||10-01-2001||07:56 PM||||Since you are already rebuilding it, there are some things you can do to significantly bump up the power at almost no extra cost! <P>Your first step should be calling or getting PAW's catalog. They sell complete Duke kits + performance parts.<P>I am going to assume you are changing the usual parts.<P>I am not sure if the DIS can take hyd. lifters, but if it can, you can get a mild cam for about $50. Even if you have to go with a performance roller cam, it may not cost much more than a stock one. <P>If you have a little extra time and a grinder, gasket matching the heads and manifolds is worth while.<P>If you decide to change the pistons, consider going with forged or at least higher compression. I know where to get brand new forged pistons set for $126. You could also use 350 Chevy piston, they can be had cheap.<P>Even if you do not do any mods to the engine, consider cloyes Alum. timming gears ($70), the factory fiber gears are crap. You could also use Mercruiser 181 timming gears. These are metal and can be bought second hand for cheap.<P>If you are having the heads redone think anout having a 3 angle valve/seat job. In my opinion this should be done to almost an head when it is rebuilt. The results were noticable on every engine I have had this done to. If it is a little too $$ for you, you can back cut the valves yourself with a drill and a dremel for free.<P>If I am going to take the time to rebuild an engine, I feel it's well worth it to do these mods, with the possible exception of the pistons depending on the app. Not only will the motor be alot peppier and fun to drive, but it is possible the gas milage would benifit also.<P>Erik-<BR>||||reg||7
Z||000006||theogre||10-01-2001||11:12 PM||||The info I have is that DIS duke is already 9:1 compression. The original PROM already has troubles with knock/ping at that... there's a TSB about the DIS PROMs... Even with the TSB proms, I'd watch how much you bump compression or you could have trouble running pump gas.<P>Before you ask, no, I'm not aware of any aftermarket PROMs for DIS motors. Unless you are into a crash coarse in chip programming you don't want to get to radical with this motor. The GM ECM PROMs will only tolerate so much change before you run into problems.<P>Stay with the roller lifter cam setup. that cam is already a bit more agressive than OE flat tipped hydraulics. The rollers also have inherantly less drag on the cam thus less power is wasted spinning the cam etc...<P>Stay with basic, proven, items. A good 3 angle valve job, clean up casting flash in the valve ports (Avoid major alterations of the ports, don't even really smooth them. just clean up the left over sharp stuff.) Put in some fresh pistons. buy it a header and ditch that crappy exhaust manifold... <P>replace all the sensors. use AC delco or Borg Warner for everything Except the O2. Use Bosch for O2.<P>Tear the TBi down and put a Borg Warner rebuild kit in it. That replaces all the seals, injector screens, and fuel pressure regulator diaphragm. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||erikred||10-02-2001||09:39 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B><BR>Before you ask, no, I'm not aware of any aftermarket PROMs for DIS motors. Unless you are into a crash coarse in chip programming you don't want to get to radical with this motor. The GM ECM PROMs will only tolerate so much change before you run into problems.<P>Stay with the roller lifter cam setup. that cam is already a bit more agressive than OE flat tipped hydraulics. The rollers also have inherantly less drag on the cam thus less power is wasted spinning the cam etc...<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ogre I respect your oppinion, but I feel (although I have not verified it personaly) that the stock computer will tollerate alot more than most people give it credit for. This was a big concern during the late eighties when computer controls were relativly new. Most computer controls have been proven to accept performance mods rather well, especialy GM vehicles. My 355 TA is running a medium cam with over 10:1 compression, fully ported, headers ignition, ect. with the stock chip and injector. It runs very nice. I have exchanged email with someone who used a 2.3 Ford turbo on his stock duke. He installed a timming retard (I think) and a Kenne Bell Boost-A-Pump. The computer was stock. He estimated over 200hp according to his times.<P>The advantage of going to a hydraulic cam (assuming the lifters fit) is the lower cost. Also, if the appropriate cam is selected, it will give more performance than the stock roller.<P>Erik-<P>||||reg||1
